1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,560
 All right, we're going to introduce you guys to some basic concepts in PCB design.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,460
 So for a lot of you, this will be the first time that you've ever designed a PCB.

3
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:10,740
 I suspect for most of you that's the case.

4
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:16,240
 And so there's a couple of important concepts that you'll want to kind of understand

5
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:22,260
 and some sort of basic terminology that you'll need to know to be able to understand what it is that you're doing.

6
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:29,540
 Now, the other video that Chandan has produced will describe how to go through and create your first design.

7
00:00:29,540 --> 00:00:34,600
 So I wanted to give a little bit of an introduction to some of the concepts and some of the things that he discusses in there.

8
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:44,640
 So first of all, the process that he's going to take you through is to go through some of the key steps in the design process,

9
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:48,100
 which essentially begin with schematic capture.

10
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:52,460
 So at this point, it's sort of assumed that you've already kind of gone through your basic design.

11
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:59,480
 You know what your circuit is, and you place your schematic into the layout tool.

12
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:01,900
 All right, so you'll do that in Orcad.

13
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:05,540
 What you then do is you get that schematic ready for layout.

14
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:10,540
 So Chandan will walk you through that process, basically how you attach package symbols,

15
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:12,520
 specifically what we call footprints.

16
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:22,400
 In other words, how we actually go about putting in the specific ways in which the chip itself or the component itself is laid out.

17
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:27,220
 So there's some basic things sort of mechanically that we need to get you introduced to to understand this piece.

18
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:29,180
 And then creating a netlist.

19
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:34,540
 So a netlist is an important element of the manufacturing process.

20
00:01:34,980 --> 00:01:42,980
 This netlist basically creates for us a list of all the nodes in the circuit and tells us where all the nodes are connected.

21
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:52,980
 Then it basically goes through setting up the PCB design environment, essentially creating some basic standards that he's going to use.

22
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:58,480
 So things like what basic line widths should be, what the vias are going to be.

23
00:01:58,480 --> 00:01:59,480
 We're going to talk a lot more about that in a minute.

24
00:01:59,480 --> 00:01:59,520
 So we're going to talk a lot more about that in a minute.

25
00:01:59,520 --> 00:01:59,520
 So we're going to talk a lot more about that in a minute.

26
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:01,360
 We're going to talk a lot more about this later on in the semester.

27
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,300
 We're just getting introduced to it right now.

28
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:07,840
 But we're going to talk a little bit about why we have some of those constraints.

29
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:13,340
 Then you're going to go through the process of actually laying out the circuit and placing the components.

30
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:16,000
 And so I guess it's really two things.

31
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:17,480
 One is you're placing the components.

32
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:22,980
 And two, you're then routing, which means that what you're doing is you're routing the nets.

33
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:28,800
 You're basically connecting the individual components with various traces, we call them.

34
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:33,160
 Then you're kind of going through your last steps to kind of prepare for manufacturing.

35
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:35,960
 So getting what's called your silkscreen ready.

36
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:37,360
 So we'll talk about what that is.

37
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:43,120
 Then basically you're going to generate the files that are used to actually create this.

38
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:46,980
 In the initial video, Chandon doesn't really talk about these elements.

39
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:48,420
 We'll talk about some of those later.

40
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,180
 But basically what we're talking about is what we call our Gerber files or artwork.

41
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:59,480
 These are the files along with our drill files that tell the manufacturer how to manufacture.

42
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:16,220
 We then generate some basic documentation, things like a bill of materials for our components so that we know what components go on there and what components we need to purchase in order to complete the actual build of the board.

43
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:19,840
 And then what we do is we submit our files for a fabrication check.

44
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:29,000
 So for that, we actually send them to the manufacturer to see if there's any issues that might prevent the board from working.

45
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:31,840
 These issues are kind of mechanical things.

46
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:44,560
 As we'll see, there still might be other issues that arise electrically that, you know, really can't get picked up by the manufacturer.

47
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:47,440
 Things that, you know, may make the circuit not work.

48
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:51,540
 But this is really meant to see if, you know, things get too close to each other.

49
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:54,800
 Here's the – these are the basic steps.

50
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:59,440
 I want to talk a little bit about some of the things that – some of the terms, I guess.

51
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:00,700
 That I used in there.

52
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:04,420
 First of all, one of the things I described was a netlist.

53
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:14,880
 We're not going to talk much about the specifics of the netlist, but it's one of the critical elements, and it's one of the steps that John Donne will take you through, what he calls netlisting.

54
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:21,480
 So in this particular case, what I see, a very simple circuit with a, you know, battery, resistor, and an LED.

55
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:25,320
 If I look carefully at this, this circuit has three nodes, right?

56
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:26,640
 It's got a ground node.

57
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,420
 It's got a node here between that battery.

58
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:32,760
 It's got a battery in the resistor, and it's got a node between the resistor and the LED.

59
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:36,660
 So what I see is this guy defines three nets.

60
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:44,620
 And you'll see in your designs these net names will appear on the wires that run around through the circuit.

61
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:48,100
 Okay, so you'll see these – you'll see names like this.

62
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:51,780
 What you have then is a net connections table down here.

63
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:53,680
 Again, I'm not going to go through the structure of this.

64
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:59,000
 But basically what this does is it describes how individual components in the circuit,

65
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:02,140
 are connected via these nodes, all right?

66
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:11,220
 So this is one of the last critical steps we do as we go from the schematic capture into the actual PCB design layout.

67
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:13,120
 All right.

68
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:19,580
 So when we get to the PCB itself, the printed circuit board, just to introduce some basic concepts, right?

69
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:24,740
 So the board itself is made up of a couple of different layers.

70
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:27,840
 What I'm showing here is a basic two-layer board.

71
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:29,380
 The critical layer.

72
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:31,560
 The two layers you have are the copper layers.

73
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:37,420
 These are where the traces are that actually are the wires within the circuit, right?

74
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:40,140
 That are connecting the different components in the circuit.

75
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:44,800
 In this two-layer board, then I have another one here on the bottom layer.

76
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:47,700
 So we'll refer to this as the top copper layer.

77
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:49,520
 Very often we just say the top layer.

78
00:05:49,980 --> 00:05:52,820
 And then the bottom copper layer, or just the bottom layer.

79
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:57,380
 Between those is a dielectric material, which is essentially fiberglass.

80
00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:58,140
 Fiberglass.

81
00:05:58,260 --> 00:05:59,360
 Sometimes called the –

82
00:05:59,360 --> 00:05:59,920
 Substrate.

83
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:05,380
 That material is referred to, that fiberglass material is referred to as FR4.

84
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:16,400
 And FR4 is, as I said, a particular type of fiberglass, has a particular dielectric set of dielectric properties and such that can be looked up.

85
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:26,100
 So typically, if you look at a basic board, what you have is copper on the top and on the bottom of one of those pieces of FR4 substrate.

86
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:37,220
 On – in addition, what most boards also have is this sort of layer on top of that copper.

87
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:41,920
 It's on top, both on the bottom and on the top, called solder mask.

88
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:43,580
 We'll take a look at that in a second.

89
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:49,480
 But what solder mask is, is basically a layer of insulation that sits on top of the copper so that things won't touch each other.

90
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:53,900
 And then very often what you do is you have a layer of silkscreen on top.

91
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:58,740
 The silkscreen is basically text that allows us to identify what the components are.

92
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:14,160
 So just as a basic example of – of what – what we might have, let's say if we had a multi-layer board, which is eventually what you guys will get to, is you basically have a sandwich of these individual boards like this.

93
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:20,420
 So what I have here is I have copper and then some FR4, and then I have a layer of copper.

94
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:25,540
 And then I have another one down here with copper, FR4, and copper.

95
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:29,320
 And in between those, I have a material called prepreg, which is a –

96
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:34,960
 a cheaper material, which – which helps the individual elements to adhere, all right?

97
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:37,540
 So this – in this particular case, I have a four-layer board.

98
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:48,360
 And we'll see, you know, that's pretty common and most likely what most of you will ultimately develop when you get into Lab 3, where you need to use more than two layers.

99
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:58,940
 Just so you have an idea, if you're looking at a motherboard in a computer or something of that level of complexity, you might have something upwards of, say, 16 layers, and it might be a pretty

100
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:00,840
 sophisticated design.

101
00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:04,980
 Most designs, you know, are somewhere between two and six layers.

102
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:08,860
 All right, now I used a couple of terms there.

103
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:15,160
 I wanted to just kind of show these things. Some of you – or hopefully all of you – have seen a PCB at some point.

104
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:21,880
 The solder mask here is that green layer of material. And if I look carefully at the board,

105
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:27,640
 what I can see, these things here are called pads, all right?

106
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:29,280
 Whenever I have exposed copper,

107
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:31,580
 like this, it has that silvery look.

108
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:38,320
 That's a pad, and that's something to which I can solder. And so in this particular case, I've got two types of pads.

109
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:43,260
 I have these sort of annular rings, which are around what I call a plated through-hole.

110
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:49,380
 So a hole that's been drilled through the board and is plated with copper all the way through the board.

111
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:54,600
 Those are the sorts of things that we typically solder to. Now, there's another type of

112
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:59,260
 through-hole that we don't solder to, and

113
00:08:59,300 --> 00:09:01,260
 that's called a via, all right?

114
00:09:01,260 --> 00:09:03,260
 And that's one of – this is a via right here.

115
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:08,580
 What that via is doing is it's connecting one layer to another.

116
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:11,280
 So it's connecting a wire on one layer to another layer.

117
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:18,900
 If I look carefully at this board, what I can see is I have these sort of, I don't know, lighter green areas,

118
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:24,180
 I guess, and then these kind of darker green areas. Where I have these lighter areas,

119
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:29,240
 that's basically where there's copper sitting on top of that FR4 substrate.

120
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:33,660
 So here I can see I've got a big copper area, right?

121
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:39,680
 This is what is sometimes called a copper pour, where copper is effectively in a large plane.

122
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:44,980
 All right, so we'll use that terminology, a copper pour. These other sort of skinnier

123
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:48,740
 areas right here, these are copper traces.

124
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:55,460
 Essentially what's been done is the silkscreen has been sat on top of the top layer,

125
00:09:55,460 --> 00:09:59,120
 and so wherever it crosses over copper, you get this sort of

126
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:04,900
 lighter color. These darker areas, basically, that's just open board, all right?

127
00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:15,060
 That's basically just the solder mask sitting on top of just blank substrate, the FR4 substrate.

128
00:10:15,060 --> 00:10:22,100
 So a couple of things here. So I said these are plated through-holes, these plated through-holes

129
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:29,100
 right here with these big annular rings around them you can solder to, those are for what we call through-hole

130
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:33,660
 components. I also have pads that are sitting right here. These pads are for

131
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:37,140
 what we call surface mount components, right? And we would solder the pins

132
00:10:37,140 --> 00:10:43,140
 directly to these pads. So we'll talk more about those. In the first lab, you're

133
00:10:43,140 --> 00:10:47,760
 going to deal mostly with surface mount components in your design, although later

134
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:50,820
 on we're going to switch over more to the boards that you're actually going to

135
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:58,480
 manufacture, and in those particular cases, we'll deal with through-hole components.

136
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:04,660
 I did mention here quickly this via. That's something we typically don't solder to. We'll

137
00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:08,760
 talk a little bit more about that here in a bit. The other element I should talk about

138
00:11:08,760 --> 00:11:15,600
 is the silkscreen. So all of this white, say this white component outline here, or this

139
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:20,700
 white text where it says RX with the plus and the minus, those are what we call silkscreen.

140
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:24,360
 And that's often a critical thing to allow us to know what components we're actually

141
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:32,260
 looking at when we're looking at the board. Now just to understand, so this is a six-layer

142
00:11:32,260 --> 00:11:38,260
 board, so this is a pretty complicated one. But just to see it in stack up, what I have

143
00:11:38,260 --> 00:11:46,260
 is basically, so one, two, three of those FR4 layers, right, where I've got copper

144
00:11:46,260 --> 00:11:50,580
 on both sides. So here's layers one and two, three and four.

145
00:11:50,580 --> 00:11:59,200
 Five and six. And what I have is holes that run through the board, okay? So I've got two

146
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:03,620
 holes that are running through the board, and then I can see some copper traces up on

147
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:09,220
 top. And it looks like in here, I've got a pretty big layer of copper. That's pretty

148
00:12:09,220 --> 00:12:13,900
 common on our inner planes. So in this particular case, in this example, it's calling that a

149
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:20,460
 ground plane. And then down here, where it's also pretty big, we see that it's called a

150
00:12:20,460 --> 00:12:26,340
 VCC plane. So we'll talk later in more detail about power distribution. But that's a pretty

151
00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:30,260
 common thing to do, to have a lot of ground plane and a lot of power plane. And then in

152
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:36,540
 addition to that, I sort of have my signal traces on the other ones.

153
00:12:36,540 --> 00:12:42,900
 I mentioned vias. Vias basically are when nets have to cross. So whenever I have to

154
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:50,340
 cross from one node over another one, what I have to do is I have to take one of those

155
00:12:50,340 --> 00:12:55,880
 and I have to change copper layers. When we do that, we make use of a via. So here's kind

156
00:12:55,880 --> 00:13:01,960
 of a simple example. What I have going on is I might have a trace on top, a wire or

157
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:09,720
 trace that then goes through a plated through hole, and then to the wire that it's going

158
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:15,100
 to go to on the layer beneath it, all right? That is a pretty common thing to do. We try

159
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:20,220
 to avoid it as much as possible through careful layout. But if we need to do it, we need to

160
00:13:20,220 --> 00:13:24,840
 do it, all right? All boards have these things. We just do our best to try to avoid them as

161
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:31,800
 much as possible through careful layout and routing, okay? So one thing about these guys

162
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:36,580
 is they, although they do have a ring around them, that ring is usually much smaller since

163
00:13:36,580 --> 00:13:43,820
 we don't need to be soldering to those particular components. Now, when you're done the final

164
00:13:43,820 --> 00:13:50,100
 process, and that's not talked about in the initial video, what you'll ultimately generate

165
00:13:50,100 --> 00:13:57,860
 is a set of artwork. Those artwork files are called Gerber files. These dot art files are

166
00:13:57,860 --> 00:14:03,340
 called Gerber files. What they do is they basically provide to the board manufacturer

167
00:14:03,340 --> 00:14:10,480
 some indication as to how they should be laying out the copper and how they should be drilling

168
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:19,980
 the boards. So if you look at this, what you have is, so it says top and bottom. So these

169
00:14:19,980 --> 00:14:24,820
 are the top and bottom copper layers, and then you've got a silkscreen on top, the solder

170
00:14:24,820 --> 00:14:34,820
 mask, the bottom piece here, and then some other elements that we'll talk about later.

171
00:14:34,820 --> 00:14:39,660
 In addition, what you have is a drill file, which basically tells the manufacturer where

172
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:48,420
 to drill holes in the XY plane, and also tells them how big those holes need to be, okay?

173
00:14:48,420 --> 00:14:49,860
 So we'll talk more about how we can identify holes.

174
00:14:49,860 --> 00:14:53,000
 We'll talk more about how we can identify those sorts of things later. These files

175
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:59,680
 ultimately are the ones or part of what gets submitted to the manufacturer and are reviewed

176
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:05,000
 when we go through something called the DRC or design rule check. So we'll talk about

177
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:10,960
 that in more detail later on. Now, when you're actually going through the design process,

178
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:16,380
 we'll talk some more about this later, there are certain standards that one uses, and a

179
00:15:16,380 --> 00:15:19,740
 common standard is IPC-2221.

180
00:15:19,740 --> 00:15:26,140
 The Generic Standard for Printed Circuit Board Design. This, as you can see, comes from this

181
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:31,860
 Institute for Interconnecting and Packaging of Electronic Circuits, IPC. There's other

182
00:15:31,860 --> 00:15:36,280
 flavors of this, but the critical elements that it outlines for us are some things that

183
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:39,800
 we need to know when we're designing boards.

184
00:15:39,800 --> 00:15:49,620
 So just as an example of what I'm kind of referring to, you know, from taking a look

185
00:15:49,620 --> 00:15:53,080
 at, let's say you're making fields, right? You know that, okay, if there's a voltage

186
00:15:53,080 --> 00:15:58,280
 on this line, let's say for sake of argument that this wire right here is at 100 volts,

187
00:15:58,280 --> 00:16:03,180
 and well, I guess in this particular case, this guy is actually grounded. I see that

188
00:16:03,180 --> 00:16:09,520
 this is connected to the ground layer, alright? So because this is ground layer and it's connected

189
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:16,240
 physically to this plated through hole and then to this line, this is a grounded line.

190
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:19,500
 Let's say this guy right here was at 100 volts, alright?

191
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:25,060
 So if there's 100 volts here and there's ground here, there's an electric field between those

192
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:31,600
 two things. There is a possibility when I have an electric field over some distance

193
00:16:31,600 --> 00:16:36,620
 like this that I can have a phenomenon called dielectric breakdown, right? Where I can actually

194
00:16:36,620 --> 00:16:42,980
 get some jump, some electrons to jump from one side to the other, right? We want to avoid

195
00:16:42,980 --> 00:16:47,840
 that. And so one of the critical things that we have is standards about what the distance

196
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:49,380
 between lines should be.

197
00:16:49,380 --> 00:16:54,620
 And that distance is largely generated by, controlled by voltage because we're trying

198
00:16:54,620 --> 00:16:59,660
 to prevent that dielectric breakdown that you talked about in fields.

199
00:16:59,660 --> 00:17:06,440
 So one of the things that's in that design standard is it gives us things like, okay,

200
00:17:06,440 --> 00:17:14,980
 well, if the voltage between conductors is between zero and 15 volts, and depending on

201
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:19,260
 where it is, whether it's on internal conductors or whether it's on the external side, so there's

202
00:17:19,260 --> 00:17:25,460
 internal layers or external layers, there's different widths that I need to maintain.

203
00:17:25,460 --> 00:17:29,500
 Notice that as the voltages go up, the distances go up.

204
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:35,940
 Now notice in this particular case, these units are in millimeters. You'll see a mix

205
00:17:35,940 --> 00:17:42,320
 and match of units when you do PCB design. Very often you see millimeters, but the other

206
00:17:42,320 --> 00:17:47,140
